I am coming back to you with the revised document:

* I am keeping the comments open and changes unaccepted simply because we will have to know what changes were made to make sure we don’t lose track of them.
* I have responded to the remaining open comments that you had - let us know if you agree. 
* There was one specific part that I wanted to discuss separately. It is in Section 7:
** *_Section 7 after your changes:_* Any personal data collected in the course of providing Bolt Services is transferred to and stored in our data centres which are located within the EEA. Only authorised employees of ​Bolt -group companies- and partners ​have access to the personal data and they may access the data to the extent necessary to comply with requests of the Passengers in connection with Bolt Services or contribute in providing services to the Passengers -they may access the data only for the purpose of resolving issues associated with the use of the services- (including disputes regarding transportation services and customer support services in the respective countries  [https://bolt.eu/cities/|https://bolt.eu/cities/|smart-link] ).
** Removing the part I crossed out might make the Notices too ambiguous regarding when and how personal data can be accessed. The new phrasing which could be interpreted in various ways, leading to potential overreach in data access. Keeping the original wording enhances transparency and builds trust with users by clearly outlining the circumstances under which their data can be accessed.
** Let us know if this part needs further discussion.

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
Other open questions/points
{panel}

* *Why among the controllers is BG Mobility but not Bolt Technology OU? Isn’t it the same case Bolt Operations OU being a subsidiary? -* Bolt Ops OU is the main controller in relation to the decisions made about personal data and how it is used. We would include Bolt Tech in the Controller List we are preparing.  {color:#4c9aff}_Understood. Just to be clear, just like in the case of Bolt Operations OU, it is Bolt ATX GmbH, and not our parent company BG Mobility Ltd., that makes decisions about personal data and how it is used._{color}

→ To our understanding, BG Mobility is the party to AT partnership agreement and therefore legally Bolt may share any data related to the market with BG Mobility. Let us know if we misunderstood the setup. 

* *Is there a need legally to disclose subsidiaries and 3rd party partners? AFAIK there is no need to do this.* - We should disclose all entities who exert some form of control (i.e. act as a controller) in respect of the data - this is why we would name Bolt Ops OU and the partner entity as they both play a role. {color:#4c9aff}_Thank you for the clarification, but let me rephrase my question. Provided that we should disclose all entities who exert control on data, is there any legal obligation to distinguish or identify those entities as being "subsidiaries" or "third-party partners"? Can't we just nominate the relevant entities associated with the respective jurisdiction without creating two separate tables?_{color}

→ There is no need to indicate the structure of ownership of certain companies (i.e. whether they are parent companies, subsidiaries, etc.). However, for example, if a subsidiary acts as a data controller, they need to be named.

